<problem>
<!-- <title>Review derivative of exponential to introduce differential equations</title> -->

<setup>

<function name="f" variables="t" symbolic="true">e^(2t)</function>
<math name="d_f" simplify="full"><derivative>$f</derivative></math>
<function name="g" variables="t" symbolic="true">e^(-5t)</function>
<math name="d_g" simplify="full"><derivative>$g</derivative></math>
<function name="h" variables="t" symbolic="true">e^(a*t)</function>
<math name="d_h" simplify="full"><derivative>$h</derivative></math>
</setup>

<!--<p>debug: f = <m>$f</m></p>
<p>debug: d_f= <m>$d_f</m></p>
<p>debug: g = <m>$g</m></p>
<p>debug: d_g= <m>$d_g</m></p>
<p>debug: h = <m>$h</m></p>
<p>debug: d_h= <m>$d_h</m></p>-->

To begin, let's review the derivative of the exponential function.




<!--Problem #1********************************************-->  
<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
If <m>f(t)=$f </m>, what is <m>\displaystyle \frac{ df }{ dt }</m>?  
<answer>
<award symbolicEquality="true" simplifyOnCompare="true">$d_f </award>
</answer>
 If <m>g(t)=$g </m>, what is <m>\displaystyle \frac{ dg }{ dt }</m>?  
<answer>
<award symbolicEquality="true" simplifyOnCompare="true">$d_g </award>
</answer>
 If <m>h(t) = $h </m> for a constant <m>a</m>, what is <m>\displaystyle \frac{ dh }{ dt }</m>? 
<answer>
<award symbolicEquality="true" simplifyOnCompare="true">$d_h </award>
</answer>
</exercise>

<!--Problem#2*********************************************-->
<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
<p>
Let's say that the function <m>u(t)</m> is one of those three functions.  It is either <m>u(t)=$f </m>, <m>u(t)=$g </m> or <m>u(t)=$h </m>.   To determine the identity of <m>u(t)</m>, you are given the information that the derivative <m>\displaystyle \frac{ du }{ dt }</m> is the same thing as two times the function itself, i.e., that <m>\displaystyle \frac{ du }{ dt } = 2 u(t)</m>.   What is <m>u(t)</m>?</p>
<p><m>u(t)=</m> 
<answer>
<award symbolicEquality="true" simplifyOnCompare="true">$f </award>
</answer>
</p>

</exercise>

<!--Problem#3*********************************************-->
<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
<p>
Let's say that the function <m>s(t)</m> is also one of those three functions.  You are given the additional information that the derivative <m>\displaystyle \frac{ ds }{ dt }</m> is the same thing as <m>-5</m> times the function itself, i.e., that <m>\displaystyle \frac{ ds }{ dt } = -5 s(t)</m>.  What is <m>s(t)</m>?</p>  
<p><m>s(t) =</m> 
<answer>
<award symbolicEquality="true" simplifyOnCompare="true">$g </award>
</answer></p>

</exercise>

<!--Problem#4**********************************************-->
<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
Equations like <m>\displaystyle \frac{ du }{ dt } = 2u(t)</m> are called <em>differential equations</em>.  We say that <m>u(t)</m> is a solution to that differential equation.   The above function <m>h(t)=$h </m> is a solution to a similar differential equation.  What is the differential equation that <m>h(t)</m> solves?

<p><m>\displaystyle \frac{ dh }{ dt } =</m> 
<answer>
<award symbolicEquality="true" simplifyOnCompare="true">a*h(t) </award>
<award symbolicEquality="true" simplifyOnCompare="true">a*h</award>
</answer>
</p>
</exercise>


<solution>


<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
<p>
<me>\displaystyle \frac{ df }{ dt } = $d_f </me><me>\displaystyle \frac{ dg }{ dt }=$d_g </me><me>\displaystyle \frac{ dh }{ dt } = $d_h </me></p>
</exercise>

<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
<p>
Of those three options, the function <m>u(t)</m> must be <m>u(t)=$f </m>.</p>
</exercise>

<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
<p>
Of those three options, the function <m>s(t)</m> must be <m>s(t)=$g </m>.</p>
</exercise>

<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
<p>
<m>\displaystyle \frac{ dh }{ dt } = a h(t)</m>.</p>
</exercise>


</solution>


</problem>